passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 36.58s \n=== features={} profile=release\n  PASS cargo check\n  PASS cargo build\n  PASS symbol parity (4 C symbols, 0 missing)\n  PASS no unresolved non-libc symbols\n  PASS differential tests: test result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s test result: ok. 22 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.70s test result: ok. 14 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 33.58s \n\nALL CONFIGURATIONS PASS","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_l325w3243n7mdjjd775vu43cromm7jnybxbdw3w5m77slpv7m6ra","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 58-line CWE-457 test case (MIT Lincoln Laboratory): `printIntPtrLine`, `bad` (the intentional uninitialized-pointer defect), `good`, and `driver`.\n\n**Phase A — configuration surface.** `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]` section and `CMakeLists.txt` has no `option()`/`target_compile_definitions`, and the C has no `#ifdef` beyond an include guard — so there is **exactly one** build configuration. I verified it under both Cargo profiles anyway, since `panic = \"abort\"` and optimization change codegen. `verify.sh` enumerates the feature power-set from `Cargo.toml` rather than hard-coding it, so it widens automatically.\n\n## Three real defects found and fixed\n\nThese were all invisible to happy-path testing, and all were fixed in the Rust:\n\n1. **Misaligned loads aborted instead of succeeding.** `*intNumber` carries a debug-only misaligned-pointer check, so any non-4-byte-aligned pointer died with `SIGABRT(6)` where C's plain `mov` loads fine.\n2. **Null loads raised the wrong signal.** My first fix (`read_unaligned`) routes through `copy_nonoverlapping`, whose debug-only *non-null* precondition made `printIntPtrLine(NULL)` abort with `SIGABRT(6)` instead of C's `SIGSEGV(11)`. Both are now handled by a bare `mov` via inline `asm!`, which carries neither precondition — exactly what GCC emits.\n3. **The `good()`/`bad()` shared stack slot wasn't shared.** This was the substantive finding. Disassembly showed GCC at `-O0` gives `bad()` and `good()` identical 16-byte frames with the `int *` local at the same offset `rbp-0x8` — so `good();bad()` **deterministically prints `5` twice** in C, because `bad()` reads back the pointer `good()` just stored. The original Rust read an ad-hoc `MaybeUninit` slot that rustc placed elsewhere (`entry_rsp-0x38` vs `-0x58`), printing garbage. Fixed by routing both through one `#[inline(never)] frame_body(init: bool)`, making the slot the same address by construction.\n\n## One honest limitation\n\n`bad()` in isolation is genuinely indeterminate — the **C itself** isn't reproducible there, printing `0`, `3`, `-2040302194`, `1420842379`, or taking `SIGSEGV` for the same call depending only on ASLR and call history. I pinned down every part of its behavior that *is* deterministic in C (rows 10, 22–25) and asserted those byte-for-byte; the 3 genuinely-arbitrary depth-mismatched paths are executed against both `.so`s with outcomes **recorded rather than asserted**, since asserting equality there would be asserting a fiction. This is flagged explicitly in `ERRORS.md` note A rather than papered over.\n\nI also found that `libloading`'s default `RTLD_LAZY` made the C's own output non-deterministic: the lazy symbol resolver's stack usage overwrites the slot `bad()` reads. The tests use `RTLD_NOW` to keep that loader artifact out of the comparison.\n\n## Results\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Symbol parity | 4/4, `comm -23` diff empty, no stubs, no untranslated C |\n| Phase B (`CONFIGS.md`) | 28/28 rows, 22 tests, ~1000 seeded randomized inputs |\n| Phase C (`ERRORS.md`) | 14/14 rows + 5 generic FFI checks, 14 tests |\n| Configurations | dev + release, both green; 5 consecutive runs, no flakiness |\n\nArtifacts: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `verify.sh`, and tests in `tests/` (which load both `.so`s through `libloading` and never call Rust directly, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrappers are under test too). `c_src/` is unmodified apart from the required `build/` directory."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":736,"cache_read_input_tokens":136392,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":736,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98667b9f-5deb-415f-ac44-08be2d2ff009","uuid":"87898962-0d8e-4479-a625-e6491d084cb1","timestamp":"2026-08-18T01:57:14.786Z"}
{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":1121823,"num_turns":65,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"98667b9f-5deb-415f-ac44-08be2d2ff009","total_cost_usd":5.179333499999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":118,"cache_creation_input_tokens":137128,"cache_read_input_tokens":4233837,"output_tokens":88191,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":36145},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":137128},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]":{"inputTokens":118,"outputTokens":88191,"cacheReadInputTokens":4233837,"cacheCreationInputTokens":137128,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":5.179333499999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"bedrock"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"not_first_party","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 58-line CWE-457 test case (MIT Lincoln Laboratory): `printIntPtrLine`, `bad` (the intentional uninitialized-pointer defect), `good`, and `driver`.\n\n**Phase A — configuration surface.** `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]` section and `CMakeLists.txt` has no `option()`/`target_compile_definitions`, and the C has no `#ifdef` beyond an include guard — so there is **exactly one** build configuration. I verified it under both Cargo profiles anyway, since `panic = \"abort\"` and optimization change codegen. `verify.sh` enumerates the feature power-set from `Cargo.toml` rather than hard-coding it, so it widens automatically.\n\n## Three real defects found and fixed\n\nThese were all invisible to happy-path testing, and all were fixed in the Rust:\n\n1. **Misaligned loads aborted instead of succeeding.** `*intNumber` carries a debug-only misaligned-pointer check, so any non-4-byte-aligned pointer died with `SIGABRT(6)` where C's plain `mov` loads fine.\n2. **Null loads raised the wrong signal.** My first fix (`read_unaligned`) routes through `copy_nonoverlapping`, whose debug-only *non-null* precondition made `printIntPtrLine(NULL)` abort with `SIGABRT(6)` instead of C's `SIGSEGV(11)`. Both are now handled by a bare `mov` via inline `asm!`, which carries neither precondition — exactly what GCC emits.\n3. **The `good()`/`bad()` shared stack slot wasn't shared.** This was the substantive finding. Disassembly showed GCC at `-O0` gives `bad()` and `good()` identical 16-byte frames with the `int *` local at the same offset `rbp-0x8` — so `good();bad()` **deterministically prints `5` twice** in C, because `bad()` reads back the pointer `good()` just stored. The original Rust read an ad-hoc `MaybeUninit` slot that rustc placed elsewhere (`entry_rsp-0x38` vs `-0x58`), printing garbage. Fixed by routing both through one `#[inline(never)] frame_body(init: bool)`, making the slot the same address by construction.\n\n## One honest limitation\n\n`bad()` in isolation is genuinely indeterminate — the **C itself** isn't reproducible there, printing `0`, `3`, `-2040302194`, `1420842379`, or taking `SIGSEGV` for the same call depending only on ASLR and call history. I pinned down every part of its behavior that *is* deterministic in C (rows 10, 22–25) and asserted those byte-for-byte; the 3 genuinely-arbitrary depth-mismatched paths are executed against both `.so`s with outcomes **recorded rather than asserted**, since asserting equality there would be asserting a fiction. This is flagged explicitly in `ERRORS.md` note A rather than papered over.\n\nI also found that `libloading`'s default `RTLD_LAZY` made the C's own output non-deterministic: the lazy symbol resolver's stack usage overwrites the slot `bad()` reads. The tests use `RTLD_NOW` to keep that loader artifact out of the comparison.\n\n## Results\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Symbol parity | 4/4, `comm -23` diff empty, no stubs, no untranslated C |\n| Phase B (`CONFIGS.md`) | 28/28 rows, 22 tests, ~1000 seeded randomized inputs |\n| Phase C (`ERRORS.md`) | 14/14 rows + 5 generic FFI checks, 14 tests |\n| Configurations | dev + release, both green; 5 consecutive runs, no flakiness |\n\nArtifacts: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `verify.sh`, and tests in `tests/` (which load both `.so`s through `libloading` and never call Rust directly, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrappers are under test too). `c_src/` is unmodified apart from the required `build/` directory.","ttft_ms":3908,"ttft_stream_ms":3458,"time_to_request_ms":35,"type":"result","duration_ms":1551330,"uuid":"812e7c10-3e28-4c73-b0cb-2e1cc8c31670"}
